  "query_preview": "/Through Thursday/...\nIssued at 1100 PM MDT Thu Oct 1 2026\n\nIt was a pleasant day across northeastern Colorado today. Sunshine \nreturned and the urban corridor only saw highs in the upper 60s \nto near 70. Tonight's temperatures will feel like fall as lows in \nthe 40s are expected in the plains with mid 20s to upper 30s in \nthe mountains and valleys. There is also the chance for some \npatchy fog/low clouds Friday morning mainly in the eastern plains,\nCheyenne Ridge, and Palmer Divide. A weak cyclone developing \novernight may also support a slight risk of fog north of Denver \nalong the I-25 corridor. \n\nThis weekend a ridge is still expected to build over eastern Utah \nand western Colorado bringing above average temperatures. Friday and \nSunday will see similar temperatures with upper 70s/lower 80s across \nthe I-25 corridor, Denver metro, and eastern plains. Lincoln County \nand the Palmer Divide will be slightly cooler in the upper 60s.\nSaturday will be a few degrees warmer area wide in the low/mid 80s \nwith some locations reaching the upper 80s. Additionally, a weak \ncold front appears to makes its way through the northeastern plains \nSaturday afternoon causing breezy conditions with gusts in the 20-30 \nkts range. This will not tame temperatures at all though as mixing \nwill still induce temperatures in the low/mid-80s. It is also worth \nit to note that elevated fire weather conditions are expected for a \nfew hours Saturday afternoon in the Cheyenne Ridge region with the \npassage of the front. \n\nMonday temperatures heat up to near record for Denver. Current \nforecasted temperature is 86 and that would put us just one degree \nshy from the daily record set in 2024 at 87 degrees. Elsewhere, we \nshould reach the mid/upper 80s while it stays slightly cooler in the \nupper 70s near Lincoln County and the Palmer Divide. Deterministic \nmodels shows a weak disturbance in the ridge that could move across \nour area Tuesday, and forecast soundings depict some moisture moving \nin aloft. This should be the greatest chance in the higher terrain \nas the disturbance moves overhead and moisture fills in. We \ncurrently have a 20-30% for an isolated shower or weak storm across \nthe high country. With a focus on the Central Mountains, such as \nClear Creek and Summit Counties. Through the end of next week the \ndry stagnant pattern looks to return as the ridge develops overhead \nonce again. Above normal temperatures are expected to continue in \nthe upper 70s and lower 80s.",
